/*! \page tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07 Transition from Example 6 to Example 7
 *
 *  \section tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_goal Goal
 *
 *  The primary goal of \ref example-07 is to introduce the observer pattern
 *  used by Tempus to provide application access during time integration.
 *  The example preserves the same \ref Thyra::ModelEvaluator-based van der Pol
 *  model, the same \ref Tempus::SolutionHistory, the same
 *  \ref Tempus::StepperForwardEuler, the same \ref Tempus::TimeStepControl,
 *  and the same application-managed time loop used in \ref example-06.
 *
 *  This example uses a tutorial-local `TutorialObserver` class.  Its method
 *  names mirror the \ref Tempus::IntegratorObserver lifecycle, but its
 *  arguments are adapted to the application-managed loop used in this
 *  tutorial.  In \ref example-08, \ref Tempus::IntegratorBasic provides the
 *  integrator context, and the production observer interface can be used
 *  directly.
 *
 *  New concepts introduced are:
 *
 *  - `TutorialObserver`
 *    - a tutorial-local observer used to demonstrate the observer pattern
 *  - `observeStartIntegrator(...)`
 *    - provides application access before the integration begins
 *  - `observeStartTimeStep(...)`
 *    - provides application access at the beginning of a time step
 *  - `observeNextTimeStep(...)`
 *    - provides application access after timestep metadata is selected
 *  - `observeBeforeTakeStep(...)`
 *    - provides application access before the stepper advances the solution
 *  - `observeAfterTakeStep(...)`
 *    - provides application access after the stepper advances the solution
 *  - `observeAfterCheckTimeStep(...)`
 *    - provides application access after the step status is checked
 *  - `observeEndTimeStep(...)`
 *    - provides application access at the end of a time step
 *  - `observeEndIntegrator(...)`
 *    - provides application access after the integration completes
 *
 *  This is the first step in separating application callbacks from the main
 *  time integration logic.  Rather than embedding output and other application
 *  actions directly in the loop body, the code now uses observer callbacks to
 *  organize access to time-integration events.
 *
 *  \section tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_same What stays the same
 *
 *  - The van der Pol model is still provided by a \ref Thyra::ModelEvaluator.
 *  - The application still manages the overall time loop directly.
 *  - The application still manages the \ref Tempus::SolutionHistory.
 *  - The Forward Euler step is still performed by
 *    \ref Tempus::StepperForwardEuler.
 *  - Timestep-size control is still handled by \ref Tempus::TimeStepControl.
 *  - The regression check follows the same pattern.
 *
 *  \section tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_snippets Selected code excerpts
 *
 *  The code excerpts below highlight the main changes needed to introduce
 *  observer-based application access.
 *
 *  <hr style="border:0; border-top:5px solid #ccc;">
 *  \subsection tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_snippet1 Define the tutorial observer
 *
 *  <b>Introduce a tutorial-local observer.</b>  The `TutorialObserver` class,
 *  defined in `TutorialObserver.hpp`, mirrors the callback structure of
 *  \ref Tempus::IntegratorObserver, but it receives the objects needed by the
 *  application-managed loop directly.
 *
 *  <b>Before</b>
 *  @code
 *  // No observer class in Example 6.
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  <b>After</b>
 *  @code
 *  // TutorialObserver.hpp
 *  namespace Tempus_Tutorial {
 *
 *  template <class Scalar>
 *  class TutorialObserver {
 *   public:
 *    void observeStartIntegrator(...);
 *    void observeStartTimeStep(...);
 *    void observeNextTimeStep(...);
 *    void observeBeforeTakeStep(...);
 *    void observeAfterTakeStep(...);
 *    void observeAfterCheckTimeStep(...);
 *    void observeEndTimeStep(...);
 *    void observeEndIntegrator(...);
 *  };
 *
 *  }  // namespace Tempus_Tutorial
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  The callback names match the lifecycle used by the production
 *  \ref Tempus::IntegratorObserver interface, but no
 *  \ref Tempus::Integrator is required in this example.
 *
 *  <hr style="border:0; border-top:5px solid #ccc;">
 *  \subsection tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_snippet2 Create the observer
 *
 *  <b>Create a TutorialObserver.</b>  The application constructs the observer
 *  before entering the time integration loop.
 *
 *  <b>Before</b>
 *  @code
 *  // Output
    cout << std::fixed;
    cout << std::setw(8)  << "index"
         << std::setw(10) << "time"
         << std::setw(12) << "x_0"
         << std::setw(12) << "x_1" << endl;

    auto currentState = solHistory->getCurrentState();
    cout << std::setw(8)  << currentState->getIndex()
         << std::setw(10) << std::setprecision(3) << currentState->getTime()
         << std::setw(12) << std::setprecision(4) << Thyra::get_ele(*(currentState->getX()), 0)
         << std::setw(12) << std::setprecision(4) << Thyra::get_ele(*(currentState->getX()), 1)
         << endl;
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  <b>After</b>
 *  @code
 *  Tempus_Tutorial::TutorialObserver<double> observer;
 *
 *  observer.observeStartIntegrator(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  <hr style="border:0; border-top:5px solid #ccc;">
 *  \subsection tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_snippet3 Add observer callbacks to the loop
 *
 *  <b>Observe key points in the time-step cycle.</b>  The main loop still
 *  performs the same operations as before, but observer callbacks now mark
 *  the important integration events.
 *
 *  <b>Before</b>
 *  @code
 *  solHistory->initWorkingState();
 *
 *  timeStepControl->setNextTimeStep(solHistory, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  stepper->takeStep(solHistory);
 *
 *  solHistory->promoteWorkingState();
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  <b>After</b>
 *  @code
 *  observer.observeStartTimeStep(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  solHistory->initWorkingState();
 *
 *  timeStepControl->setNextTimeStep(solHistory, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  observer.observeNextTimeStep(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  observer.observeBeforeTakeStep(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  stepper->takeStep(solHistory);
 *
 *  observer.observeAfterTakeStep(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  observer.observeAfterCheckTimeStep(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *
 *  solHistory->promoteWorkingState();
 *
 *  observer.observeEndTimeStep(
 *    solHistory, stepper, timeStepControl, integratorStatus);
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  The time loop is still application-controlled, but application access is
 *  now organized around observer callbacks.  Although there appears to be
 *  back-to-back observer calls, in Tempus::IntegratorBasic there additional
 *  calls that have not been introduced in this tutorial which separate these
 *  observer call.
 *
 *  Note that these observer functions are no-ops in this tutorial,
 *  but show where these function are called for developers usage.
 *
 *  <hr style="border:0; border-top:5px solid #ccc;">
 *  \subsection tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_snippet4 Move output into the observer
 *
 *  <b>Move application output into the observer.</b>  The step output that was
 *  previously embedded directly in the time loop is now handled by
 *  `observeEndTimeStep(...)`.
 *
 *  <b>Before</b>
 *  @code
 *  if (solHistory->getCurrentState()->getIndex() % 100 == 0) {
 *    auto currentState = solHistory->getCurrentState();
 *    cout << currentState->getIndex() << "  " << currentState->getTime()
 *         << "  " << Thyra::get_ele(*(currentState->getX()), 0)
 *         << "  " << Thyra::get_ele(*(currentState->getX()), 1) << endl;
 *  }
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  <b>After</b>
 *  @code
 *  void observeEndTimeStep(...)
 *  {
 *    auto currentState = solHistory->getCurrentState();
 *
 *    if (currentState->getIndex() % 100 == 0) {
 *      cout << currentState->getIndex()
 *           << "  " << currentState->getTime()
 *           << "  " << Thyra::get_ele(*(currentState->getX()), 0)
 *           << "  " << Thyra::get_ele(*(currentState->getX()), 1)
 *           << endl;
 *    }
 *  }
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  This illustrates how observers can separate application-specific actions
 *  from the time-integration algorithm.
 *
 *  The observers here primarily access the solution state to print out
 *  the index, time and solution.  However users can perform any other
 *  tasks (e.g., perform auxiliary calculations, add additional constraints
 *  for step failure) within observers via access to the solution
 *  history, stepper, time step control, and integrator status).
 *  A word of caution: although allowed, one should carefully consider
 *  modifying the solution history, stepper or time step control,
 *  as this could lead to inaccurate or even wrong solutions.
 *
 *  <hr style="border:0; border-top:5px solid #ccc;">
 *  \section tempus_tutorial_transition_06_07_compare How to compare the examples
 *
 *  A more detailed comparison can be made by diffing:
 *  - `examples/06_Intro_TimeStepControl/06_Intro_TimeStepControl.cpp`
 *  - `examples/07_Intro_Observers/07_Intro_Observers.cpp`
 *
 *  From the `packages/tempus` directory, a focused comparison of the main
 *  time-integration logic between these two examples can be generated locally
 *  in `bash` or `zsh` with:
 *
 *  @code{.sh}
 *  git diff --no-index \
 *    <(sed -n '91,180p' examples/06_Intro_TimeStepControl/06_Intro_TimeStepControl.cpp) \
 *    <(sed -n '88,180p' examples/07_Intro_Observers/07_Intro_Observers.cpp)
 *  @endcode
 *
 *  This ignores leading header lines (e.g., `#include` statements and
 *  Doxygen comments) and trailing regression-testing lines.
 *
 *  <hr style="border:0; border-top:5px solid #ccc;">
 *  \htmlonly
 *  <div style="text-align:center;">
 *    <a href="example-06.html">← Previous Example</a> |
 *    <a href="example-07.html">Current Example</a> |
 *    <a href="example-08.html">Next Example →</a>
 *  </div>
 *  \endhtmlonly
 */
